.md
## 9. An Euler-Lagrange trap

Before varying anything sixteen ways, one piece of Wolfram behaviour has to be pinned down,
because getting it wrong costs a wrong answer rather than an error message.

The naive field-theory Euler-Lagrange operator,

`D[lag, q[t,x]] - D[D[lag, Derivative[1,0][q][t,x]], t] - D[D[lag, Derivative[0,1][q][t,x]], x]`

does not reliably return what it looks like it returns. On the test case below it gives
**zero** where the answer is $-\partial_x^2 B$. The gradient term is exactly the one that
carries $k^2$, so a Lagrangian full of correct physics silently produces equations with no
Poisson term in them &mdash; which is what happened here on the first attempt, and what the
diagonal-tetrad diagnosis in section 8 was almost mistaken for.

The cure is to substitute the derivatives for plain symbols before differentiating, and put
them back afterwards. `EulerLagrange2D` below does that, and the cell tests both versions
against a Lagrangian whose Euler-Lagrange equation can be written down by hand.

The single-variable operator used for the background in section 6 is not affected, which is
why the Friedmann equations above were right.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 9. An Euler-Lagrange trap

Before varying anything sixteen ways, one piece of Wolfram behaviour has to be pinned down,
because getting it wrong costs a wrong answer rather than an error message.

The naive field-theory Euler-Lagrange operator,

`D[lag, q[t,x]] - D[D[lag, Derivative[1,0][q][t,x]], t] - D[D[lag, Derivative[0,1][q][t,x]], x]`

does not reliably return what it looks like it returns. On the test case below it gives
**zero** where the answer is $-\partial_x^2 B$. The gradient term is exactly the one that
carries $k^2$, so a Lagrangian full of correct physics silently produces equations with no
Poisson term in them &mdash; which is what happened here on the first attempt, and what the
diagonal-tetrad diagnosis in section 8 was almost mistaken for.

The cure is to substitute the derivatives for plain symbols before differentiating, and put
them back afterwards. `EulerLagrange2D` below does that, and the cell tests both versions
against a Lagrangian whose Euler-Lagrange equation can be written down by hand.

The single-variable operator used for the background in section 6 is not affected, which is
why the Friedmann equations above were right.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ClearAll[EulerLagrangeNaive, EulerLagrange2D];

EulerLagrangeNaive[lag_, q_] :=
  D[lag, q[t, x]] - D[D[lag, Derivative[1, 0][q][t, x]], t]
    - D[D[lag, Derivative[0, 1][q][t, x]], x];

EulerLagrange2D[lag_, q_] :=
 Module[{u0, u1, u2, fwd, back, ls},
  fwd  = {Derivative[1, 0][q][t, x] -> u1, Derivative[0, 1][q][t, x] -> u2, q[t, x] -> u0};
  back = {u0 -> q[t, x], u1 -> Derivative[1, 0][q][t, x], u2 -> Derivative[0, 1][q][t, x]};
  ls   = lag /. fwd;
  (D[ls, u0] /. back) - D[D[ls, u1] /. back, t] - D[D[ls, u2] /. back, x]];

Module[{toy, want},
 toy  = D[uA[t, x], x] D[uB[t, x], x];
 want = -D[uB[t, x], {x, 2}];
 Dataset @ {
   <|"operator" -> "naive", "result" -> EulerLagrangeNaive[toy, uA],
     "correct" -> (Simplify[EulerLagrangeNaive[toy, uA] - want] === 0)|>,
   <|"operator" -> "EulerLagrange2D", "result" -> EulerLagrange2D[toy, uA],
     "correct" -> (Simplify[EulerLagrange2D[toy, uA] - want] === 0)|>}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 10. All sixteen components

Write the tetrad as background plus $\epsilon$ times sixteen free functions of $t$ and $x$,

$$e^A{}_\mu = \bar e^A{}_\mu + \epsilon\, h^A{}_\mu(t,x),$$

and truncate at $O(\epsilon^2)$ at every step. Nothing then has to invert or expand a fully
general symbolic tetrad: the inverse and the determinant are written as explicit series and
checked against the exact ones.

The linear field equations are the Euler-Lagrange equations of the **second order** action.
That is not a shortcut, it is an identity: with $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}^{(0)} + \epsilon\mathcal{L}^{(1)} + \epsilon^2\mathcal{L}^{(2)}$
and $\delta/\delta e = \epsilon^{-1}\delta/\delta h$, the $\mathcal{L}^{(1)}$ term gives the
background equations and the $\mathcal{L}^{(2)}$ term gives their linearisation.

Keeping $h$ general until after the variation matters. A single complex plane wave substituted
early would put $e^{2ikx}$ into $\mathcal{L}^{(2)}$, and the phases no longer cancel; varying
first and specialising afterwards avoids that entirely.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 10. All sixteen components

Write the tetrad as background plus $\epsilon$ times sixteen free functions of $t$ and $x$,

$$e^A{}_\mu = \bar e^A{}_\mu + \epsilon\, h^A{}_\mu(t,x),$$

and truncate at $O(\epsilon^2)$ at every step. Nothing then has to invert or expand a fully
general symbolic tetrad: the inverse and the determinant are written as explicit series and
checked against the exact ones.

The linear field equations are the Euler-Lagrange equations of the **second order** action.
That is not a shortcut, it is an identity: with $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}^{(0)} + \epsilon\mathcal{L}^{(1)} + \epsilon^2\mathcal{L}^{(2)}$
and $\delta/\delta e = \epsilon^{-1}\delta/\delta h$, the $\mathcal{L}^{(1)}$ term gives the
background equations and the $\mathcal{L}^{(2)}$ term gives their linearisation.

Keeping $h$ general until after the variation matters. A single complex plane wave substituted
early would put $e^{2ikx}$ into $\mathcal{L}^{(2)}$, and the phases no longer cancel; varying
first and specialising afterwards avoids that entirely.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ClearAll[trunc2, tetFull, ivFull, detFull, torsionScalarFull, lagFirst, lagSecond];

trunc2[e_] := Normal[Series[e, {eps, 0, 2}]];

Module[{bgT, ibgT, hp, eup, gg, ig, dpar, tor, tDn, tUp12, tUp23, kon, tr1c, sup, mm},
 bgT  = DiagonalMatrix[{1, a[t], a[t], a[t]}];
 ibgT = DiagonalMatrix[{1, 1/a[t], 1/a[t], 1/a[t]}];
 hp   = Table[hh[i, j][t, x], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];

 tetFull = bgT + eps hp;
 ivFull  = ibgT - eps ibgT . hp . ibgT + eps^2 ibgT . hp . ibgT . hp . ibgT;
 mm      = ibgT . hp;
 detFull = Det[bgT] (1 + eps Tr[mm] + eps^2 (Tr[mm]^2 - Tr[mm . mm])/2);

 eup = Table[ivFull[[m, capA]], {capA, 4}, {m, 4}];
 gg  = Table[trunc2[Sum[$eta[[capA, capB]] tetFull[[capA, i]] tetFull[[capB, m]],
     {capA, 4}, {capB, 4}]], {i, 4}, {m, 4}];
 ig  = Table[trunc2[Sum[$eta[[capA, capB]] eup[[capA, i]] eup[[capB, m]],
     {capA, 4}, {capB, 4}]], {i, 4}, {m, 4}];

 dpar[f_, mu_] := If[mu <= 2, D[f, {t, x}[[mu]]], 0];   (* nothing depends on y or z *)

 tor = Table[trunc2[Sum[eup[[capA, l]] (dpar[tetFull[[capA, i]], m]
      - dpar[tetFull[[capA, m]], i]), {capA, 4}]], {l, 4}, {m, 4}, {i, 4}];
 tDn   = Table[trunc2[Sum[gg[[l, q]] tor[[q, m, i]], {q, 4}]], {l, 4}, {m, 4}, {i, 4}];
 tUp12 = Table[trunc2[Sum[ig[[m, p]] ig[[i, q]] tDn[[p, q, l]], {p, 4}, {q, 4}]],
    {m, 4}, {i, 4}, {l, 4}];
 tUp23 = Table[trunc2[Sum[ig[[m, p]] ig[[i, q]] tDn[[l, p, q]], {p, 4}, {q, 4}]],
    {l, 4}, {m, 4}, {i, 4}];
 kon = Table[-(1/2) (tUp12[[m, i, l]] - tUp12[[i, m, l]] - tUp23[[l, m, i]]),
    {m, 4}, {i, 4}, {l, 4}];
 tr1c = Table[trunc2[Sum[tUp12[[al, i, al]], {al, 4}]], {i, 4}];
 sup = Table[(1/2) (kon[[m, i, l]] + KroneckerDelta[m, l] tr1c[[i]]
      - KroneckerDelta[i, l] tr1c[[m]]), {l, 4}, {m, 4}, {i, 4}];

 torsionScalarFull = trunc2[Sum[sup[[l, m, i]] tor[[l, m, i]], {l, 4}, {m, 4}, {i, 4}]];
];

$T0 = Simplify[torsionScalarFull /. eps -> 0];
With[{lg = trunc2[detFull F[torsionScalarFull]]},
 lagFirst  = Coefficient[lg, eps, 1];
 lagSecond = Coefficient[lg, eps, 2]];

Dataset @ {
 <|"check" -> "the series inverse is exact to O(eps^2)",
   "ok" -> (Simplify[trunc2[tetFull . ivFull] - IdentityMatrix[4]] === ConstantArray[0, {4, 4}])|>,
 <|"check" -> "the series determinant is exact to O(eps^2)",
   "ok" -> (Simplify[trunc2[Det[tetFull]] - detFull] === 0)|>,
 <|"check" -> "background torsion scalar is 6 H^2",
   "ok" -> (Simplify[$T0 - 6 a'[t]^2/a[t]^2] === 0)|>,
 <|"check" -> "the second order Lagrangian carries spatial gradients",
   "ok" -> (Length[Cases[lagSecond, Derivative[0, 1][hh[__]][t, x], Infinity]] > 0)|>}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 11. The perturbed tetrad, the matter source, and the background on shell

The scalar sector of a perturbed tetrad in Newtonian gauge carries four functions, and two of
them are invisible to the metric:

| | |
|---|---|
| $\psi$ | the lapse perturbation, $g_{00} = -(1+2\psi)$ |
| $\phi$ | the isotropic spatial perturbation, $g_{ij} = a^2(1-2\phi)\delta_{ij}$ |
| $\zeta$ | a **boost**, $h^0{}_1 = a\zeta$ and $h^1{}_0 = \zeta$ |
| $\chi$ | a **rotation**, $h^2{}_3 = -h^3{}_2 = a\chi$ |

$\zeta$ and $\chi$ are exactly the local Lorentz directions: an infinitesimal boost with
parameter $\lambda$ sends $\delta e^0{}_1 = \lambda a$ and $\delta e^1{}_0 = \lambda$, which is
$\zeta$ on the nose. In TEGR they are gauge and must drop out of every equation. In
$f(\mathcal{T})$ they do not, and that is where the modification enters.

Matter couples through the metric, so its contribution to the tetrad equations is
$\delta S_m/\delta e^A{}_\mu = \lambda_m\, e\, e_{A\nu}T^{\mu\nu}$, and the constant is fixed by
demanding the background reproduce the Friedmann equation of section 7.

**The background has to be put on shell.** Linearised gauge invariance holds only there, so
without imposing the background equations the boost mode does not drop out even in TEGR. That
was the second thing this calculation got wrong before it got it right.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 11. The perturbed tetrad, the matter source, and the background on shell

The scalar sector of a perturbed tetrad in Newtonian gauge carries four functions, and two of
them are invisible to the metric:

| | |
|---|---|
| $\psi$ | the lapse perturbation, $g_{00} = -(1+2\psi)$ |
| $\phi$ | the isotropic spatial perturbation, $g_{ij} = a^2(1-2\phi)\delta_{ij}$ |
| $\zeta$ | a **boost**, $h^0{}_1 = a\zeta$ and $h^1{}_0 = \zeta$ |
| $\chi$ | a **rotation**, $h^2{}_3 = -h^3{}_2 = a\chi$ |

$\zeta$ and $\chi$ are exactly the local Lorentz directions: an infinitesimal boost with
parameter $\lambda$ sends $\delta e^0{}_1 = \lambda a$ and $\delta e^1{}_0 = \lambda$, which is
$\zeta$ on the nose. In TEGR they are gauge and must drop out of every equation. In
$f(\mathcal{T})$ they do not, and that is where the modification enters.

Matter couples through the metric, so its contribution to the tetrad equations is
$\delta S_m/\delta e^A{}_\mu = \lambda_m\, e\, e_{A\nu}T^{\mu\nu}$, and the constant is fixed by
demanding the background reproduce the Friedmann equation of section 7.

**The background has to be put on shell.** Linearised gauge invariance holds only there, so
without imposing the background equations the boost mode does not drop out even in TEGR. That
was the second thing this calculation got wrong before it got it right.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ClearAll[perturbedTetrad, matterSource, backgroundOnShell];

Module[{ph, trunc1, hp, ibgT, ivp, edn, gpert, detp, uUp, rhoTot, tUpper, sigmaFull,
        bgGrav, bgSrc, bgTT, bgXX},
 ph = Exp[I k x];
 trunc1[e_] := Normal[Series[e, {eps, 0, 1}]];

 perturbedTetrad = {
   {1 + eps psi[t] ph,   eps a[t] zeta[t] ph, 0, 0},
   {eps zeta[t] ph,      a[t] (1 - eps phi[t] ph), 0, 0},
   {0, 0, a[t] (1 - eps phi[t] ph),  eps a[t] chi[t] ph},
   {0, 0, -eps a[t] chi[t] ph,       a[t] (1 - eps phi[t] ph)}};

 ibgT = DiagonalMatrix[{1, 1/a[t], 1/a[t], 1/a[t]}];
 hp   = Coefficient[perturbedTetrad, eps, 1];
 ivp  = ibgT - eps ibgT . hp . ibgT;
 edn  = Table[trunc1[Sum[$eta[[capA, capB]] perturbedTetrad[[capB, m]], {capB, 4}]],
    {capA, 4}, {m, 4}];
 gpert = Table[trunc1[Sum[$eta[[capA, capB]] perturbedTetrad[[capA, i]] perturbedTetrad[[capB, m]],
      {capA, 4}, {capB, 4}]], {i, 4}, {m, 4}];
 detp = trunc1[Det[perturbedTetrad]];

 uUp    = {1 - eps psi[t] ph, eps vv[t] ph, 0, 0};
 rhoTot = rho[t] (1 + eps del[t] ph);
 tUpper = Table[trunc1[rhoTot uUp[[i]] uUp[[j]]], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];

 matterSource = Table[
   trunc1[(-2 \[Kappa]) detp Sum[edn[[capA, nu]] tUpper[[m, nu]], {nu, 4}]], {capA, 4}, {m, 4}];

 bgGrav = Table[EulerLagrange2D[lagFirst, hh[i, j]], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];
 bgSrc  = Table[Simplify[matterSource[[i, j]] /. eps -> 0], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];
 bgTT = Simplify[bgGrav[[1, 1]] - bgSrc[[1, 1]]];
 bgXX = Simplify[bgGrav[[2, 2]] - bgSrc[[2, 2]]];
 backgroundOnShell = Simplify[First@Solve[{bgTT == 0, bgXX == 0}, {rho[t], a''[t]}]];

 Column[{
   Row[{Style["metric from the tetrad, g_00 = ", Bold], gpert[[1, 1]]}],
   Row[{Style["                       g_0x = ", Bold], Simplify[gpert[[1, 2]]]}],
   Row[{Style["                       g_11 = ", Bold], gpert[[2, 2]]}],
   Dataset @ {
    <|"check" -> "the ansatz is in Newtonian gauge, g_0x = 0",
      "ok" -> (Simplify[gpert[[1, 2]]] === 0)|>,
    <|"check" -> "zeta and chi are invisible to the metric at first order",
      "ok" -> FreeQ[Simplify[Coefficient[gpert, eps, 1]], chi | zeta]|>,
    <|"check" -> "the four-velocity is normalised to O(eps)",
      "ok" -> (Simplify[trunc1[Sum[gpert[[i, j]] uUp[[i]] uUp[[j]], {i, 4}, {j, 4}]] + 1] === 0)|>,
    <|"check" -> "the background 0-0 equation is (f - 2 T f_T) = 2 kappa rho",
      "ok" -> (Simplify[bgTT/a[t]^3 - ((F[$T0] - 2 $T0 F'[$T0]) - 2 \[Kappa] rho[t])] === 0)|>}}]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 12. The linear equations

Vary the second order action sixteen ways, substitute the scalar ansatz, subtract the matter
source, drop time derivatives of the perturbation amplitudes, and put the background on shell.

The TEGR limit is the acceptance test, and it is a demanding one: the $00$ equation has to come
out as the Poisson equation with a $k^2$, the transverse equation has to force $\phi = \psi$,
the two momentum equations have to be the same equation, and both Lorentz modes have to
disappear. All four are checked below rather than asserted.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 12. The linear equations

Vary the second order action sixteen ways, substitute the scalar ansatz, subtract the matter
source, drop time derivatives of the perturbation amplitudes, and put the background on shell.

The TEGR limit is the acceptance test, and it is a demanding one: the $00$ equation has to come
out as the Poisson equation with a $k^2$, the transverse equation has to force $\phi = \psi$,
the two momentum equations have to be the same equation, and both Lorentz modes have to
disappear. All four are checked below rather than asserted.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ClearAll[linearEquations, quasiStatic];

quasiStatic = {Derivative[_][psi][t] -> 0, Derivative[_][phi][t] -> 0,
               Derivative[_][zeta][t] -> 0, Derivative[_][chi][t] -> 0,
               Derivative[_][del][t] -> 0, Derivative[_][vv][t] -> 0};

Module[{ansatz, ph, gravEq, grav},
 ph = Exp[I k x];
 ansatz = {hh[1, 1] -> Function[{t, x}, psi[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[1, 2] -> Function[{t, x}, a[t] zeta[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[2, 1] -> Function[{t, x}, zeta[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[2, 2] -> Function[{t, x}, -a[t] phi[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[3, 3] -> Function[{t, x}, -a[t] phi[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[4, 4] -> Function[{t, x}, -a[t] phi[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[3, 4] -> Function[{t, x}, a[t] chi[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[4, 3] -> Function[{t, x}, -a[t] chi[t] Exp[I k x]],
   hh[1, 3] -> Function[{t, x}, 0], hh[1, 4] -> Function[{t, x}, 0],
   hh[2, 3] -> Function[{t, x}, 0], hh[2, 4] -> Function[{t, x}, 0],
   hh[3, 1] -> Function[{t, x}, 0], hh[4, 1] -> Function[{t, x}, 0],
   hh[3, 2] -> Function[{t, x}, 0], hh[4, 2] -> Function[{t, x}, 0]};

 gravEq = Table[EulerLagrange2D[lagSecond, hh[i, j]], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];
 grav   = Table[Simplify[(gravEq[[i, j]] /. ansatz)/ph], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];

 linearEquations = Table[
   Simplify[Simplify[(grav[[i, j]]
        - Coefficient[matterSource[[i, j]], eps, 1]/ph) /. quasiStatic]
      /. backgroundOnShell], {i, 4}, {j, 4}];
];

Module[{tegr},
 tegr = Simplify[linearEquations /. F -> Function[u, -u]];
 Column[{
  Row[{Style["TEGR  0-0 : ", Bold], tegr[[1, 1]], " = 0"}],
  Row[{Style["TEGR  0-1 : ", Bold], tegr[[1, 2]], " = 0"}],
  Row[{Style["TEGR  1-0 : ", Bold], tegr[[2, 1]], " = 0"}],
  Row[{Style["TEGR  1-1 : ", Bold], tegr[[2, 2]], " = 0"}],
  Row[{Style["TEGR  2-2 : ", Bold], tegr[[3, 3]], " = 0"}],
  Row[{Style["TEGR  2-3 : ", Bold], tegr[[3, 4]], " = 0"}],
  "",
  Dataset @ {
   <|"acceptance test" -> "0-0 is the Poisson equation, carrying k^2",
     "ok" -> (Exponent[Expand[tegr[[1, 1]]], k] === 2)|>,
   <|"acceptance test" -> "the transverse equation forces phi = psi, no slip",
     "ok" -> (Simplify[tegr[[3, 3]] /. phi[t] -> psi[t]] === 0 && tegr[[3, 3]] =!= 0)|>,
   <|"acceptance test" -> "the two momentum equations are the same equation",
     "ok" -> (Simplify[tegr[[2, 1]] + a[t] tegr[[1, 2]]] === 0)|>,
   <|"acceptance test" -> "the boost mode zeta drops out, as local Lorentz demands",
     "ok" -> FreeQ[tegr, zeta]|>,
   <|"acceptance test" -> "the rotation mode chi drops out",
     "ok" -> FreeQ[tegr, chi]|>,
   <|"acceptance test" -> "the pressure equation vanishes for dust",
     "ok" -> (tegr[[2, 2]] === 0)|>}}]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 13. The effective gravitational coupling

Now solve. The $0$-$1$ equation gives the peculiar velocity; the $1$-$0$ equation then becomes
a leftover constraint which vanishes identically in TEGR &mdash; that is local Lorentz
invariance showing up as an algebraic identity &mdash; and for a non-linear $f$ it is the
equation that fixes the boost mode. Together with the transverse equation it determines $\phi$
and $\zeta$ in terms of $\psi$, and the $0$-$0$ equation then reads off against

$$\frac{k^2}{a^2}\Psi = -4\pi G_{\text{eff}}\,\rho_m\delta_m .$$

The answer, in the sub-horizon limit, is

$$
\frac{G_{\text{eff}}}{G} = -\frac{1}{f_{\mathcal{T}}}, \qquad \eta = \frac{\Phi}{\Psi} = 1 .
$$

With $\mathcal{T} = +6H^2$ here, TEGR is $f = -\mathcal{T}$ and $f_\mathcal{T} = -1$, giving
$G_{\text{eff}} = G$. In the GR-02 and GR-04 convention, where `Ts` $= -\mathcal{T}$ and TEGR is
$f(\texttt{Ts}) = \texttt{Ts}$, the same statement reads $G_{\text{eff}}/G = 1/f_{\texttt{Ts}}$
&mdash; which is exactly the entry GR-04's library had been quoting from the literature. It is
no longer quoted.

Two features are worth naming. It is **scale independent**: no $k$ survives, in sharp contrast
to $f(R)$, where GR-05 found a factor of $4/3$ running between large and small scales. And
there is **no gravitational slip**, so lensing and clustering feel the same modification,
where in $f(R)$ they do not. Those two differences are what would let a survey tell the
theories apart even if their backgrounds agreed exactly.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 13. The effective gravitational coupling

Now solve. The $0$-$1$ equation gives the peculiar velocity; the $1$-$0$ equation then becomes
a leftover constraint which vanishes identically in TEGR &mdash; that is local Lorentz
invariance showing up as an algebraic identity &mdash; and for a non-linear $f$ it is the
equation that fixes the boost mode. Together with the transverse equation it determines $\phi$
and $\zeta$ in terms of $\psi$, and the $0$-$0$ equation then reads off against

$$\frac{k^2}{a^2}\Psi = -4\pi G_{\text{eff}}\,\rho_m\delta_m .$$

The answer, in the sub-horizon limit, is

$$
\frac{G_{\text{eff}}}{G} = -\frac{1}{f_{\mathcal{T}}}, \qquad \eta = \frac{\Phi}{\Psi} = 1 .
$$

With $\mathcal{T} = +6H^2$ here, TEGR is $f = -\mathcal{T}$ and $f_\mathcal{T} = -1$, giving
$G_{\text{eff}} = G$. In the GR-02 and GR-04 convention, where `Ts` $= -\mathcal{T}$ and TEGR is
$f(\texttt{Ts}) = \texttt{Ts}$, the same statement reads $G_{\text{eff}}/G = 1/f_{\texttt{Ts}}$
&mdash; which is exactly the entry GR-04's library had been quoting from the literature. It is
no longer quoted.

Two features are worth naming. It is **scale independent**: no $k$ survives, in sharp contrast
to $f(R)$, where GR-05 found a factor of $4/3$ running between large and small scales. And
there is **no gravitational slip**, so lensing and clustering feel the same modification,
where in $f(R)$ they do not. Those two differences are what would let a survey tell the
theories apart even if their backgrounds agreed exactly.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
ClearAll[geffExact, geffSubHorizon, slipSubHorizon];

Module[{nm, e00, e01, e10, exx, eyy, eyz, vsol, lorentz, sol, poi, dsol, abbrev},
 abbrev = {Derivative[3][F][$T0] -> fTTT, Derivative[2][F][$T0] -> fTT,
           Derivative[1][F][$T0] -> fT, F[$T0] -> f0};
 nm[e_] := Simplify[Expand[e] /. abbrev];

 e00 = nm[linearEquations[[1, 1]]];  e01 = nm[linearEquations[[1, 2]]];
 e10 = nm[linearEquations[[2, 1]]];  exx = nm[linearEquations[[2, 2]]];
 eyy = nm[linearEquations[[3, 3]]];  eyz = nm[linearEquations[[3, 4]]];

 vsol    = First@Solve[e01 == 0, vv[t]];
 lorentz = Simplify[e10 /. vsol];
 sol     = Simplify[First@Solve[{lorentz == 0, eyy == 0}, {phi[t], zeta[t]}]];
 poi     = Simplify[(e00 /. vsol) /. sol];
 dsol    = First@Solve[poi == 0, del[t]];

 geffExact = Simplify[
   ((k^2 psi[t]/a[t]^2)/(-(\[Kappa]/2) rho[t] (del[t] /. dsol))) /. backgroundOnShell /. abbrev];
 geffSubHorizon = Simplify[Limit[geffExact, k -> Infinity]];
 slipSubHorizon = Simplify[
   Limit[Simplify[((phi[t] /. sol)/psi[t]) /. backgroundOnShell /. abbrev], k -> Infinity]];

 Column[{
   Row[{Style["the leftover 1-0 constraint : ", Bold], Short[lorentz, 3]}],
   Row[{Style["G_eff / G, sub-horizon      : ", Bold], geffSubHorizon}],
   Row[{Style["slip  Phi/Psi, sub-horizon  : ", Bold], slipSubHorizon}]}]]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 14. Checks

Everything the notebook claims. The geometry checks come first, then the background, then the
perturbative result. The last group is the one that matters: if any of it fails, the
$G_{\text{eff}}$ above is not to be believed.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
## 14. Checks

Everything the notebook claims. The geometry checks come first, then the background, then the
perturbative result. The last group is the one that matters: if any of it fails, the
$G_{\text{eff}}$ above is not to be believed.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: false
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Module[{ys = {t, r, th, ph}, f1, s1, d1, tv, grSub, p1, p2},
 f1 = Teleparallel[DiagonalMatrix[{1, a[t], a[t], a[t]}], xs];
 s1 = Teleparallel[DiagonalMatrix[{Sqrt[1 - 2 M/r], 1/Sqrt[1 - 2 M/r], r, r Sin[th]}], ys];
 d1 = Teleparallel[DiagonalMatrix[{1, Exp[hh0 t], Exp[hh0 t], Exp[hh0 t]}], xs];
 p1 = Teleparallel[DiagonalMatrix[{1, 1, r, r Sin[th]}], ys];
 p2 = Teleparallel[{{1, 0, 0, 0},
     {0, Sin[th] Cos[ph], r Cos[th] Cos[ph], -r Sin[th] Sin[ph]},
     {0, Sin[th] Sin[ph], r Cos[th] Sin[ph], r Sin[th] Cos[ph]},
     {0, Cos[th], -r Sin[th], 0}}, ys];
 tv = 6 a'[t]^2/a[t]^2;
 grSub = {fT -> -1, fTT -> 0, fTTT -> 0, f0 -> -tv};

 Dataset @ {
  <|"check" -> "T = 6 H^2 in flat FLRW",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[f1["TorsionScalar"] - tv] === 0)|>,
  <|"check" -> "superpotential and three-term forms agree on three tetrads",
    "ok" -> AllTrue[{f1, s1, d1},
       Simplify[#["TorsionScalar"] - #["TorsionScalarAlt"]] === 0 &]|>,
  <|"check" -> "R = -T + B on all three",
    "ok" -> AllTrue[{{f1, xs}, {s1, ys}, {d1, xs}},
       Simplify[RicciScalarOf[#[[1]]["Metric"], #[[2]]]
          - (-#[[1]]["TorsionScalar"] + BoundaryTerm[#[[1]]])] === 0 &]|>,
  <|"check" -> "the good-tetrad problem: same metric, different T",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[p1["Metric"] - p2["Metric"]] === ConstantArray[0, {4, 4}] &&
       Simplify[p1["TorsionScalar"] - p2["TorsionScalar"]] =!= 0)|>,
  <|"check" -> "the naive Euler-Lagrange operator really does drop the gradient term",
    "ok" -> (EulerLagrangeNaive[D[uA[t, x], x] D[uB[t, x], x], uA] === 0)|>,
  <|"check" -> "EulerLagrange2D gets it right",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[EulerLagrange2D[D[uA[t, x], x] D[uB[t, x], x], uA]
        + D[uB[t, x], {x, 2}]] === 0)|>,
  <|"check" -> "TEGR: G_eff = G",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[geffSubHorizon /. grSub] === 1)|>,
  <|"check" -> "G_eff = -1/f_T, and is scale independent",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[geffSubHorizon - (-1/fT)] === 0 && FreeQ[geffSubHorizon, k])|>,
  <|"check" -> "no gravitational slip",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[slipSubHorizon - 1] === 0)|>,
  <|"check" -> "this is what GR-04's library quotes, in its own sign convention",
    "ok" -> (Simplify[(geffSubHorizon /. fT -> -fTs) - 1/fTs] === 0)|>}]

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
## 15. What is still quoted

$f(\mathcal{T})$ is now derived end to end in this series: geometry here, background here,
$G_{\text{eff}}$ here, growth in GR-04. Together with $f(R)$ in GR-05 that leaves one gap.

$f(Q)$ remains quoted. Its fundamental variable is a flat, torsion-free connection, and the
coincident gauge that makes $Q$ come out as the tidy $-6H^2$ used in GR-02 is a statement about
that connection rather than about coordinates. It does not stack for free on top of Newtonian
gauge, so a consistent calculation either keeps the connection perturbations explicitly or
gives up the convenient form of $Q$. The machinery in sections 10 to 13 is the right shape for
it &mdash; general perturbation, second order action, all components free, background on shell,
TEGR-style limit as the acceptance test &mdash; but the variables are different.

Both families also carry known perturbative pathologies: extra modes propagate and their
kinetic terms can degenerate on cosmological backgrounds, the strong-coupling problem. A
correct linear calculation is necessary, not sufficient.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
